      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p class="texte-petit texte-2 note-mesure">
          Two of the four accented capitals can still be typed on the traditional AZERTY:
          <strong>È</strong> and <strong>À</strong> come from the grave accent dead key,
          <kbd>AltGr</kbd>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<kbd>7</kbd> followed by the vowel, and that legend is
          printed on keyboards sold in France. <strong>É</strong> and <strong>Ç</strong> have no
          sequence: you need an Alt code or a copy and paste. None of the four can be typed in one keystroke.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>Impact on typing, in numbers</h2>

      <div class="tableau-compare tableau-compare--compact bloc-mesure">
        <table role="table">
          <caption>Two measures: a count, then the same count weighted by real-world usage.</caption>
          <thead role="rowgroup">
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col">Measure</th>
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col"><span class="entete-long">AZERTY </span>Global</th>
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col">AFNOR<span class="entete-long"> AZERTY</span></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody role="rowgroup">
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row">Existing characters moved</th>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AZERTY Global">13.8%</td>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AFNOR AZERTY">30.3%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row">Share of keystrokes affected</th>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AZERTY Global">1.01%</td>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AFNOR AZERTY">4.50%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p class="texte-petit texte-2 note-mesure">
          The first row counts the characters that move. The second takes into account how often
          they appear in French text: moving a rare character changes fewer keystrokes.
        </p>
        <details class="notice" id="methode-comparatif">
          <summary>Sources and calculation method</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu">
            <p>Of the 109 characters on the traditional Windows AZERTY, 15 change key or level with AZERTY&nbsp;Global, against 33 with AFNOR. The share of keystrokes affected is calculated on the frequencies of the AFNOR corpus (text half formal, half informal). It depends on the type of text typed.</p>
            <p>The layout definitions and the calculations are available in the <a href="https://github.com/AMCF-asso/azerty-global-website">source repository of the site</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>Why AZERTY&nbsp;Global rather than AFNOR?</h2>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Since Windows&nbsp;11 24H2, the AFNOR AZERTY ships with the
          system</strong>, under the name “French (Standard, AZERTY)”. The historical AZERTY is now
          “French (Traditional, AZERTY)”. You select the standard in Settings, with no installation
          and no administrator rights.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tableau-compare tableau-compare--compact bloc-mesure">
        <table role="table">
          <caption>The cost of the transition, counted character by character.</caption>
          <thead role="rowgroup">
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col">What you have to relearn</th>
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col"><span class="entete-long">AZERTY </span>Global</th>
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col">AFNOR<span class="entete-long"> AZERTY</span></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody role="rowgroup">
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row">Characters affected</th>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AZERTY Global">15</td>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AFNOR AZERTY">33</td>
            </tr>
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row">That change key</th>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AZERTY Global">12</td>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AFNOR AZERTY">31</td>
            </tr>
            <tr role="row">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row">Distinct destination keys</th>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AZERTY Global">12</td>
              <td role="cell" data-colonne="AFNOR AZERTY">22</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p class="texte-petit texte-2 note-mesure">
          The 15 AZERTY&nbsp;Global characters are the five announced changes, counted one by
          one. On an AZERTY keyboard, 31 keycap legends become wrong with AFNOR,
          against 12 with AZERTY&nbsp;Global. <a href="#methode-comparatif">See the calculation method</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Two gaps remain, regardless of key positions. The standard ships with the system only
          from Windows&nbsp;11 24H2: not Windows&nbsp;10, not macOS, not Linux.
          On those last two, it exists only as <strong>drivers that users built and that you install
          yourself</strong>, listed on
          <a href="https://norme-azerty.fr" hreflang="fr">norme-azerty.fr<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.
          AZERTY&nbsp;Global provides layout files for all three systems.
          Its Windows app also includes lessons, a virtual keyboard and a character search.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="temoignage citation-afnor">
        <blockquote>
          <p>“I tested the standardized AFNOR AZERTY for months. Then I tested
          AZERTY&nbsp;Global for 2 days. The winner: AZERTY&nbsp;Global, by far.
          Adjusting is super fast and it improves everything else.”</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>Gelth, IT professional (Twitter, 2023)</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>Where do the moved characters end up?</h2>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          AFNOR does have a logic: it groups characters by category.
          AZERTY&nbsp;Global groups them by gesture, and a rule is easier to remember than
          isolated positions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cartes-regles">
        <div class="carte">
          <h3>How AZERTY&nbsp;Global groups characters</h3>
          <ul class="liste-filet">
            <li><strong>AltGr + the home row</strong> for the six programming symbols, grouped
              in pairs: the braces <code>{</code> <code>}</code> on D and F, the backslash and the
              vertical bar <code>\</code> <code>|</code> on G and H, the brackets
              <code>[</code> <code>]</code> on J and K.</li>
            <li><strong><code>@</code> and <code>#</code> on the key at the very top
              left</strong>: the at sign direct, the hash with Shift. And the old
              AltGr + 0 still gives <code>@</code>.</li>
            <li><strong><code>%</code> with Shift on the key to the right of 0</strong>: it sits
              next to the digits. The <code>°</code> stays on that key.</li>
            <li><strong>Three of the most-used dead keys are grouped on the ù
              key</strong>: acute, grave and tilde. The circumflex and the diaeresis keep their
              usual key.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="carte">
          <h3>What the AFNOR AZERTY moves</h3>
          <ul class="liste-filet">
            <li><strong>The period and the comma swap keys.</strong> The comma key gives the
              period, the period key gives the comma, and the semicolon moves to the exclamation
              mark key. These are the most frequent punctuation marks in French, and the
              best-ingrained gesture.</li>
            <li><strong>The apostrophe and the straight double quote move to the very top
              right</strong>, onto the parenthesis key to the right of 0: the apostrophe
              direct, the double quote with Shift.</li>
            <li>The same six programming symbols move to <strong>six keys across three
              rows</strong>: <code>{</code> on AltGr + T, <code>}</code> on AltGr + Y,
              <code>[</code> on AltGr + 5, <code>]</code> on AltGr + 6, <code>\</code> on
              Shift + Ù, <code>|</code> on AltGr + L.</li>
            <li><strong><code>à</code> moves from the 0 key to the 1 key</strong>,
              <code>è</code> from the 7 key to the 3 key, and <code>%</code> moves to AltGr + P.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>What about bépo, Optimot and Ergo-L?</h2>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Bépo, Optimot and Ergo-L are <strong>ergonomic layouts</strong>:
          they rearrange the letters to reduce finger movement.
          Adopting one means relearning how to type: several weeks, even several
          months, before you get your speed back.
          AZERTY&nbsp;Global keeps the letters A to Z where they are and changes how you reach accents and symbols.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cartes-dispositions">
        <div class="carte">
          <h3><a href="https://bepo.fr" hreflang="fr">bépo<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></h3>
          <p>
            A free layout derived from the Dvorak method, developed by a community since
            2005 and adopted by AFNOR in 2019 as the second layout of the NF&nbsp;Z71-300
            standard, alongside the standardized AZERTY. Entirely reorganized alphabet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="carte">
          <h3><a href="https://optimot.fr" hreflang="fr">Optimot<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></h3>
          <p>
            Created by Patrick Jamet and developed since 2022, optimized jointly for
            French and English, with common characters reachable without AltGr.
            Published under the CC&nbsp;BY-NC-SA license.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="carte">
          <h3><a href="https://ergol.org" hreflang="fr">Ergo-L<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></h3>
          <p>
            An original design drawing on the QWERTY-Lafayette and bépo projects, at version 1.0.0
            since May 2024. Optimized in equal parts for French, English and code, with a
            dead key that keeps all the French letters on the three central rows.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose bilan-ergonomiques">
        <p>
          To keep your typing habits for letters, choose AZERTY&nbsp;Global.
          If you want to learn a new letter arrangement to reduce finger movement,
          compare bépo, Optimot and Ergo-L.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>Common questions about choosing a layout</h2>

      <div class="faq-liste">
        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Do I need to buy a keyboard or stickers?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              No. AZERTY&nbsp;Global is purely software and your current keyboard
              works: <strong>no keyboard with AZERTY&nbsp;Global
              legends exists yet</strong>, and none is needed. The letters and digits do not move,
              so your keycap legends stay right for the most part; three keys change meaning
              (<kbd>;</kbd> <kbd>²</kbd> <kbd>ù</kbd>), and the AltGr symbols are on the cheat sheet.
              <strong>Four printable sticker sheets</strong> are provided, one of
              which shows the old and the new placement side by side. No sticker set
              is sold today: where to find them and how to print them is
              <a href="/en/faq">in the FAQ</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>AZERTY Global or the US International QWERTY?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The US International layout is the usual answer for English speakers who need é and ñ.
              It works until you write French for real. The apostrophe is a dead key, so
              <em>don’t</em> and <em>it’s</em> take <kbd>'</kbd> followed by a space, dozens of
              times a day. There is no œ ligature (<em>cœur</em>, <em>sœur</em>,
              <em>œuvre</em>), and the French quotation marks « » sit behind shortcuts few
              people know (<kbd>AltGr</kbd>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<kbd>[</kbd> and <kbd>]</kbd>). Keyboards
              in France are physically AZERTY, so the printed keycaps do not match a US
              International layout.
            </p>
            <p>
              AZERTY Global keeps the AZERTY base used in France and fixes its real flaws:
              accented capitals with <kbd>Caps Lock</kbd>, programming symbols on the home row,
              a direct period, plus dead-key accents for Spanish, German, Portuguese,
              Polish and more than 300 other languages. Coming from a US layout, you relearn
              the letter positions of the keyboard used in France.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>AZERTY Global or the AZERTY of macOS?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Apple’s AZERTY is a step ahead of the Windows one, and
              AZERTY&nbsp;Global takes its best choice: the at sign direct at the very top
              left, the hash with Shift on the same key. The gaps remain clear. The period still
              needs the Shift key. The braces, brackets, backslash and vertical bar are
              scattered across Option combinations spread over three rows, and are printed on no
              key. AZERTY&nbsp;Global groups them in pairs on the home row.
              AZERTY&nbsp;Global also installs on macOS.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>AZERTY Global or the fr-oss layout on Linux?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The fr-oss layout is the closest to AZERTY&nbsp;Global: it already has the smart
              Caps Lock for É È Ç À, the French quotation marks on AltGr&nbsp;+&nbsp;W
              and AltGr&nbsp;+&nbsp;X, and the œ on AltGr&nbsp;+&nbsp;O, in the same
              places. Three gaps remain: the period still needs the Shift key, the at sign
              stays on AltGr&nbsp;+&nbsp;0, and the programming symbols stay scattered over the
              number row instead of the home row. And fr-oss exists only on Linux, whereas
              AZERTY&nbsp;Global also covers Windows and macOS.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Do I have to relearn how to type with AZERTY Global?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              No. All the letters and all the digits stay in place. Fifteen characters
              change, twelve of them changing key, and the share of keystrokes affected is
              1.01% on the frequencies of the AFNOR corpus (text half formal, half informal):
              99% of the keystrokes of a French text stay unchanged.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="vision">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>Our vision</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Our goal: to offer a pragmatic alternative that works for as many people as possible.
          The traditional Windows AZERTY is more than 40&nbsp;years old and was never designed to
          write French properly. AFNOR took a step in the right direction, but 31&nbsp;characters
          change key and its programming symbols remain hard to reach. AZERTY&nbsp;Global fixes the
          real problems while keeping everything that already works.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

